# AvePoint assumptions

Before analyzing or changing this case study, read
**[docs/ASSUMPTIONS.md](../../docs/ASSUMPTIONS.md)** for the cutoff, horizon,
cohort rule, and — most importantly — the load-bearing choice of `churn_events`
as ground truth over `churn_flag`.

Column-level provenance and point-in-time safety:
**[docs/DATA_DICTIONARY.md](../../docs/DATA_DICTIONARY.md)**.

## The one thing to know before touching the target

The three recorded churn signals — `accounts.churn_flag`, a row in
`churn_events`, and a subscription with an `end_date` — are **mutually
unrelated**, not merely inconsistent. Every pair sits on its own chance baseline
(κ = −0.016, +0.024, +0.065; p = 0.56, 0.45, 0.14).

Raw agreement is the wrong instrument here and invites a wrong conclusion: the
sources fire at 22%, 70.4% and 61.4%, so unrelated columns already agree ~39% of
the time. "37.6% agreement" is chance, not "worse than a coin flip", and
inverting `churn_flag` does not recover a signal either.

Computed by `src.audit.label_source_agreement` and
`src.audit.churn_date_coherence`; pinned by tests in `tests/test_analyses.py`.
Do not restate the agreement figures without their chance baselines.

## The one thing to know before reporting a time trend

**Churn dates are a uniform draw between `signup_date` and `EXTRACT_DATE`**
(KS p = 0.92 pooled, and inside every signup quarter). Because no date can land
after the end of the extract, that draw produces a hazard of `1 / (END − t)` —
a large, exquisitely significant rising trend on data where nothing happened.

It manufactures every timing pattern in this dataset at once: the ×2.8/yr
calendar rise, the cohort gradient, and the tenure effect behind the onboarding
case. All three are **withdrawn findings**. `src/generator.py` and
`notebooks/16_generator_audit.py` reproduce them from the draw alone — observed
rate ratio 1.0893 against a null of 1.0885, at the 52nd percentile of pure noise.

Do not reinstate "churn is rising, find what changed in 2024". The relevant null
on this data is never "is the effect flat" but **"would the file produce this on
its own"**, and `generator.calendar_hazard_null` is how that is tested.

Related: `feature_usage.usage_date` and `support_tickets.submitted_at` are
uniform over the whole extract and correlate with their own account's signup
date at r = 0.002 and r = 0.014. Those tables cannot carry a signal about the
accounts they are attached to, at any sample size.
